  "hints": [
    "Whenever you find an unvisited cell == \"1\", increment regions += 1 and flood-fill all orthogonally connected \"1\" neighbors.",
    "Do NOT mutate the caller grid (keep a `visited` set) and use an iterative stack or deque BFS so large grids never hit Python recursion limits.",
    "Pseudocode:\n  visited = set(); regions = 0\n  for r in range(R): for c in range(C):\n      if grid[r][c] == \"1\" and (r, c) not in visited:\n          regions += 1; flood_bfs(r, c)\n  return regions"
